                                 <p><strong>How do lenders decide on Personal Loan eligibility?</strong><br>

                                    <br>Lenders evaluate several factors to determine loan eligibility. These criteria help them assess the borrower's creditworthiness and ability to repay the loan. While specific eligibility requirements may vary among lenders, the following factors are typically considered:

                                 <div class="overviewbullet">

                                    <ul>

                                       <li class="flex items-start gap-3 my-2"><span class="flex-shrink-0 mt-1"><img src="/img/flexible-repayment-tenure.svg" width="28" height="28" alt="Credit Score: A credit score reflects the borrower’s credit history and repayment track record. A higher credit score indicates a lower risk for lenders, increasing the chances of loan approval." class="w-7 h-7 object-contain" /></span><div><strong>Credit Score: </strong>A credit score reflects the borrower’s credit history and repayment track record. A higher credit score indicates a lower risk for lenders, increasing the chances of loan approval.</div></li>

                                       <li class="flex items-start gap-3 my-2"><span class="flex-shrink-0 mt-1"><img src="/img/employment.svg" width="28" height="28" alt="Income and Employment Stability: Lenders assess the borrower's income level and stability to ensure they have a regular source of income to meet repayment obligations." class="w-7 h-7 object-contain" /></span><div><strong>Income and Employment Stability: </strong>Lenders assess the borrower's income level and stability to ensure they have a regular source of income to meet repayment obligations.</div></li>

                                       <li class="flex items-start gap-3 my-2"><span class="flex-shrink-0 mt-1"><img src="/img/flexible-repayment-tenure.svg" width="28" height="28" alt="Debt-to-Income Ratio: This ratio compares a borrower's monthly debt obligations to their monthly income. Lenders prefer a lower debt-to-income ratio, indicating that the borrower has sufficient income to manage additional loan repayments." class="w-7 h-7 object-contain" /></span><div><strong>Debt-to-Income Ratio: </strong>This ratio compares a borrower's monthly debt obligations to their monthly income. Lenders prefer a lower debt-to-income ratio, indicating that the borrower has sufficient income to manage additional loan repayments.</div></li>

                                       <li class="flex items-start gap-3 my-2"><span class="flex-shrink-0 mt-1"><img src="/img/income.svg" width="28" height="28" alt="Loan Amount and Tenure: The loan amount and tenure also affect eligibility. Lenders may have specific criteria regarding the maximum loan amount they are willing to offer based on the borrower's income and financial situation. Additionally, longer tenures may impact eligibility, as lenders consider the borrower's age and retirement plans." class="w-7 h-7 object-contain" /></span><div><strong>Loan Amount and Tenure: </strong>The loan amount and tenure also affect eligibility. Lenders may have specific criteria regarding the maximum loan amount they are willing to offer based on the borrower's income and financial situation. Additionally, longer tenures may impact eligibility, as lenders consider the borrower's age and retirement plans.</div></li>

                                       <li class="flex items-start gap-3 my-2"><span class="flex-shrink-0 mt-1"><img src="/img/documents-table-arrow.svg" width="28" height="28" alt="Existing Financial Obligations: Lenders evaluate the borrower's existing debt, such as credit card balances, other loans, or EMIs. These obligations impact the borrower's ability to take on additional debt and may affect their eligibility." class="w-7 h-7 object-contain" /></span><div><strong>Existing Financial Obligations: </strong>Lenders evaluate the borrower's existing debt, such as credit card balances, other loans, or EMIs. These obligations impact the borrower's ability to take on additional debt and may affect their eligibility.</div></li>

